WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
<v Speaker 1>Hello both, thanks for jumping on. So, I've got news on Willowmere Cottage.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.200
<v Speaker 3>Oh God, okay, is it good news?

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.667
<v Speaker 2>Let him talk, love.

00:00:11.867 --> 00:00:16.867
<v Speaker 1>Ha. It's mixed. So the vendors have come back on your offer.

00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:18.467
<v Speaker 2>Right.

00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:25.000
<v Speaker 1>You offered $7M. They've said they'd accept, um, a bit more. They want $735,000.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.667
<v Speaker 3>That's, what, like four percent more?

00:00:28.867 --> 00:00:31.733
<v Speaker 2>Bit under. Three and a half.

00:00:31.933 --> 00:00:42.000
<v Speaker 1>About that, yes. And, between us, I think they'll go lower, they're going through a divorce and they need it sold before the court date.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:47.200
<v Speaker 2>Ah. That's, um. That's useful to know. Bit grim, but useful.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.467
<v Speaker 1>I didn't say it.

00:00:49.667 --> 00:00:51.533
<v Speaker 3>Say what? Ha.

00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:57.067
<v Speaker 1>Ha. Exactly. So, how are you both feeling about the house itself?

00:00:57.267 --> 00:01:04.133
<v Speaker 3>I love it. I love it. The garden, the little shed, the view over to the Malvern Hills...

00:01:04.333 --> 00:01:07.267
<v Speaker 2>The survey's what worries me.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:15.467
<v Speaker 1>Yes. So the surveyor flagged some damp in the back bedroom, and the roof's, um, got maybe ten years left.

00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:18.333
<v Speaker 2>And the subsidence thing.

00:01:18.533 --> 00:01:23.800
<v Speaker 1>Historic. Historic subsidence. It's been monitored, it's stable.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.733
<v Speaker 3>What does that mean for, like, insurance?

00:01:27.933 --> 00:01:37.933
<v Speaker 1>Premiums can be a bit higher. Maybe fifteen to twenty percent above normal. Hartfield Mutual are usually decent for that sort of thing.

00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:44.133
<v Speaker 2>Okay. And the mortgage? We're with Kingsmead Building Society in principle.

00:01:44.333 --> 00:01:47.200
<v Speaker 1>What rate did they give you?

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.867
<v Speaker 2>34% fixed, five years.

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:57.267
<v Speaker 1>That's good. That's really good, actually. I've seen people at five and a quarter this month.

00:01:57.467 --> 00:02:01.133
<v Speaker 3>We put down ten percent. Is that enough?

00:02:01.333 --> 00:02:06.200
<v Speaker 1>For that rate, yes. More is always better, but ten's fine.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:18.133
<v Speaker 2>We were going to put down more, but my company's just announced a restructure and I don't know yet whether I've got a job in January, so we're keeping some back.

00:02:18.333 --> 00:02:20.267
<v Speaker 1>Very sensible.

00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:26.467
<v Speaker 3>We're also, um, selling the place in Corran Falls. My flat. Well, our flat.

00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:29.533
<v Speaker 1>Oh, right, how's that going?

00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:37.267
<v Speaker 3>Slowly. The buyer's mortgage fell through because apparently he lied about his income, which, wow.

00:02:37.467 --> 00:02:41.133
<v Speaker 1>Oof. That happens more than you'd think.

00:02:41.333 --> 00:02:44.867
<v Speaker 2>Can I ask about the area? The schools?

00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:52.667
<v Speaker 1>Sure. Primary's rated outstanding, in the village. Secondary is the one in Grisbeck, which is fine.

00:02:52.867 --> 00:02:54.533
<v Speaker 3>And crime?

00:02:54.733 --> 00:03:00.400
<v Speaker 1>Very low. The only thing anyone complains about is the bypass traffic.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.933
<v Speaker 2>And there's no plans to build on the field behind?

00:03:05.133 --> 00:03:15.267
<v Speaker 1>Not that I know of. I'd check with the council though, between us the farmer who owns it has been quietly talking to a housing developer.

00:03:15.467 --> 00:03:17.533
<v Speaker 3>Ooh. Neighbours!

00:03:17.733 --> 00:03:19.933
<v Speaker 2>Not helpful, love.

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:24.667
<v Speaker 1>Ha. So, what would you like me to say to the vendors?

00:03:24.867 --> 00:03:29.800
<v Speaker 2>Let's say $55M, and we can complete by the end of February.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.733
<v Speaker 3>Can we say mid-February? I want to be in by Valentine's.

00:03:34.933 --> 00:03:37.533
<v Speaker 2>That's not how it works.

00:03:37.733 --> 00:03:45.267
<v Speaker 1>Ha. I'll say end of February, with flexibility. And I'll push the damp as a reason for the number.

00:03:45.467 --> 00:03:46.800
<v Speaker 2>Good.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:51.933
<v Speaker 1>Can I just get your solicitor's details again for the memo?

00:03:52.133 --> 00:04:00.800
<v Speaker 2>Yes, it's Penrose Fairclough, and the contact is Lazarus Tolliver. Her email is ravindra_brackenridge@postbox.test.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:04.133
<v Speaker 1>Lovely. And best number for you?

00:04:04.333 --> 00:04:05.933
<v Speaker 2>555-0168.

00:04:06.133 --> 00:04:09.933
<v Speaker 3>Or mine, I always pick up! (353) 555-0142.

00:04:10.133 --> 00:04:16.333
<v Speaker 1>Ha, noted. Okay, I'll come back to you by tomorrow afternoon. Fingers crossed.

00:04:16.533 --> 00:04:18.667
<v Speaker 3>Fingers and toes!

00:04:18.867 --> 00:04:20.667
<v Speaker 2>Thanks. Bye.
